---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Questions from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/Questions.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import questions from "../data/questions.json";

// Work list row 58, 17 September 2026. This page was 166 lines of hand-written
// prose with the questions inlined as a const. They are data now —
// src/data/questions.json — in the shape kit/components/Questions.astro reads:
// the question, WHAT IS KNOWN, WHAT HAS BEEN TRIED, and the record that would
// settle it. The kit component had been sitting unused because only two of the
// seven archives held their questions as data. This one now does.
//
// Nothing was summarised in the move: the split was checked word by word
// against the prose it replaced, and the only things that did not carry over
// were connectives and tense.
//
// These bodies are hand-written HTML, so their hrefs need the site base
// prefixed the same way u() does it. Applying it to only one field left five
// links broken in the built site once already, so it is applied to every field
// that can hold one.
const base = (html) => String(html || "").replace(/href='\//g, `href='${u("/")}`);
const rows = questions.rows.map((r) => ({
  ...r,
  q: base(r.q), rank: base(r.rank), known: base(r.known),
  tried: base(r.tried), settles: base(r.settles),
}));
const open = rows.filter((r) => r.state !== "answered").length;
---
<Base title="Open Questions"
  eyebrow={`${open} open · ${rows.length - open} answered`}
  dek="Written down so they can be worked on, and so no reader mistakes a gap for a fact. Each one says what would actually close it."
>
  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">The most valuable thing anyone can send this archive is a
    <strong>correction</strong>. The second most valuable is a document that closes one of
    these.</p>
    <p class="dek"><strong>Each question is in four parts</strong> — what is known, what has been
    tried, and the record that would settle it. The middle one is the part most archives leave
    out, and it is the part that stops the same dead end being walked twice.</p>
  </section>

  <Questions rows={rows} u={u} unit="questions" />

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Drafted, not sent</div>
    <h2>Three letters</h2>
    <p class="lede">Three of these questions can only be answered by a book that is not online. The
    enquiries are written and sit in <code class="mono">requests/</code>, each naming exactly what
    is wanted and what is already known, so the search can be narrow. <strong>None has been
    sent.</strong></p>
    <table class="kv">
      <tr><th>Rijeka,<br />Senj branch</th><td><strong>The registers of Sv. Jakov, Krmpote</strong> —
        the parish named by Toma's 1907 death entry, and therefore the book holding every Blažević
        above him. Wanted: <strong>Jurko's baptism</strong> (likely 1855–1870), his
        <strong>marriage to Tereza Žubrinić</strong> (likely 1885–92), or his <strong>death</strong>
        before December 1920. Any one of the three names his father and ends
        <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>question 1b</a>.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Rijeka,<br />Senj branch</th><td><strong>Senj, 1915 — the death of Zvonimir
        Vukelić</strong>: how Milka came to be widowed at twenty-eight, about which this archive
        knows nothing at all.</td></tr>
      <tr><th><strong>Gospić</strong><br /><span class="dim">added 10 Sept 2026</span></th>
        <td><strong>Otočac — one man called Michaël Žubrinić, or two?</strong> The 1834 baptism at
        Šumećica 24 and the 1880 son named at his 1910 marriage give parents of the
        <em>same two names</em> forty-six years apart. Wanted: a
        <strong>Žubrinić–Orešković marriage before 1834</strong>, and whether a
        <strong>second one exists around 1855–70</strong>. One entry, or its absence, decides four
        generations. <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Question 4d</a>.</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1.1rem"><strong>Two of the three were first addressed to the
    wrong archive.</strong> Gospić covers Lika-Senj <em>“osim gradova Senja i Novalje”</em> —
    except the towns of Senj and Novalja — and Smokvica Krmpotska is not in Lika-Senj at all.
    Senj, Krmpote and Krivi Put belong to <strong>Rijeka</strong>; Otočac and Karlobag to
    <strong>Gospić</strong>. <a href={u("/corrections/")}>That correction →</a></p>
    <p class="dek">All three drafts say plainly that <strong>a nil return is worth having</strong>
    and will be recorded as one. An archive that holds nothing is a finding; an archive never asked
    is not.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>And the standing one</h2>
    <p class="note">Everything above generation eight is <strong>silence</strong>, not mystery. The
    Croatian parish registers for this coast begin in the sixteenth century and many survive; the
    Blažević line simply has not been followed into them below <strong>Mateša Blažević, born about
    1750</strong>. That is a research gap, and it is closable by someone with the patience to read
    Latin parish hands. <a href={u("/how-far-back/")}>How far back →</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
